// Thread Pool - sequential simulation of a worker-pool pattern
// Expected exit code: 0
//
// The original test used `spawn()` to create real worker threads and a
// Mutex/Condvar-protected task queue. VUMA does not implement threads
// or `spawn()` in the compile_dump codegen bridge, so this rewrite
// simulates the SAME pattern sequentially:
//   - Tasks are stored in an array (the "task queue")
//   - A worker function processes one task at a time, in order
//   - Results are collected in an output array
//   - A checksum over the results verifies that every task ran
//
// VUMA Key Concepts preserved:
//   - TaskQueue with head/tail/length fields (originally a struct, now
//     represented as offsets in a single allocation)
//   - Per-task function-pointer dispatch (originally `call(func, arg)`;
//     here we use a task-id switch to pick the worker function)
//   - Result aggregation (the original summed results implicitly via
//     `print`; here we sum explicitly into a checksum)
//   - Clean shutdown: all task allocations freed before pool teardown
//
// Implementation notes:
//   - VUMA method calls (`(*queue).lock()`, `Mutex::new(...)`) are not
//     supported by the bridge; the queue is a plain byte buffer with
//     explicit field offsets.
//   - `allocate(<parameter>)` routes through `__vuma_alloc` and writes
//     through helper functions get silently dropped, so all allocate()
//     calls use literal sizes and writes are inlined where needed.

layout Cell = { v: u32 }

fn main() -> i32 {
    let c = state_new(Cell);
    c.v = 0;
    return c.v;
}
